# Adjournment Motion: Reply on the Fires in Madras
Tamil title: ஒத்திவைப்புத் தீர்மானம் - பதிலுரை (சென்னை நகரில் தீ விபத்துகள்)

Speech 17, dated 5 July 1967

Subject: Adjournment motion - reply concerning the fires in Madras city

The Honourable C. N. Annadurai:

Mr Deputy Speaker, the fires that have occurred repeatedly in Madras city over the past several days have deeply distressed the minds of all the people. It is painful to reflect that those affected are poor and vulnerable people, that they are suffering after incurring enormous losses, and that some have lost their lives. Nevertheless, we must consider whether the proceedings of the House need to be adjourned in order to communicate our anguish to the people and provide relief to those affected by the fires.

If we meet to consider what must be done to provide relief to the people there, and if each of us acts individually and collectively after the House concludes, relief work can proceed very quickly. Mr M. P. Sivagnanam has put forward a proposal worthy of serious attention: instead of various organisations working from different directions for the relief of the affected people, every kind of organisation and private individual could be brought together and the work carried out through the government itself.

It is embarrassing even to consider whether a conspiracy lies behind these fires. For it would make us ashamed of ourselves merely to admit that society might contain people so depraved. If there are conspiracies, we ask the Police Department to engage its full ability in the effort to discover them. If such antisocial persons do exist, I state that they will be punished without the slightest mercy. A general rumour is circulating that the fires occurred as part of a conspiracy, but no definite conclusion is yet known.

The police have already taken certain steps to ensure public safety. Police officers are travelling in vans to areas inhabited by poor people and reassuring them that appropriate protection will be provided. Residents of those poor hut settlements are being told not to admit into their homes anyone with whom they have no prior acquaintance and to inform the police immediately. In addition, plainclothes police officers are patrolling at various places and watching for anyone engaging in such antisocial acts. Adequate attention is therefore being given to this matter, and the measures have now been made still more vigorous.

Our Leader of the Opposition said that fire-service personnel went away saying that a key was unavailable for an hour and then returned, causing many houses to be destroyed. If details are provided identifying the particular fire engine and the personnel concerned, this government is prepared to censure and correct them appropriately. If the statement merely repeats something heard from someone else, it would not carry the same weight.

We must also take into account that such hut settlements in Madras are built with extreme density and that, when an accident occurs, there is insufficient access to reach it immediately and contain it. These circumstances increase the severity of the consequences. Whenever such accidents occur, this government is prepared to provide through the government whatever is required to enable fire-service personnel to work with still greater speed.

The Leader of the Opposition said that those who lost their homes in these fires should be given at least one hundred rupees in assistance. I thank him for generally asking that even more be provided. Under the government in which his party participated, each family affected by fire was given only thirty rupees, with another ten rupees for clothing. Last week I ordered that this amount be raised: sixty rupees for each family and twenty rupees for clothing, making a total of eighty rupees. I suspect that the Opposition leaders may have somehow anticipated that the government would announce eighty rupees and therefore suggested one hundred rupees.

If we consider whether even one hundred rupees is sufficient for a family affected by fire, it certainly is not. I therefore ask each of us to accept responsibility for encouraging legislators and public organisations to come forward and provide relief and assistance. If every member devotes serious effort to this during the time remaining outside our working hours, I believe it can certainly be accomplished. The proceedings of the House need not be adjourned for this purpose.

Many tasks are being carried out efficiently at the place where the fire occurred. Those who remain far away from the fire site need not postpone the proceedings of the House. If the honourable member agrees, when the business of the House ends at half past eleven and the House rises, all of us can go together to the fire-affected area and spend two hours there. If the members are prepared to do so, I am ready to follow them.

---NOTES---
The source has no literary headline. Its printed subject line is retained as the Tamil title and translated as the English title.

The opening of Image 2 and much of Image 4 are badly fragmented in OCR. Direct inspection of the scans restores the continuous sentences concerning shame at possible antisocial conduct, police investigation, punishment, and the proposed joint visit after the House rose.

The source says the fire-service personnel reported that “a key was unavailable for an hour.” The precise operational context is not explained, so the translation preserves the statement without speculation.

The monetary amounts are historical rupee figures stated in the speech and are not adjusted for inflation.

Claims concerning the fires, rumours of conspiracy, policing, relief rates, and earlier government practice are translated in the speaker's parliamentary voice and have not been independently verified.
